.TH LWM 1
.SH NAME
lwm \- Lightweight Window Manager for the X Window System
.SH SYNTAX
\fBlwm \fP[\fB\-s\fP session-id] [\fB\-debugcli\fP] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fILwm\fP is a window manager for the X Window System.  It provides enough
features to allow the user to manage their windows, and no more.
.PP
Windows are surrounded by a frame with a titlebar at the top next to a close
icon (in the form of a cross).  The window with input focus is coloured
differently from the others.
.PP
\fILwm\fP uses the sloppy-focus scheme by default, whereby moving the pointer
into a window gives it input focus, but moving the pointer out does not cause
focus to be lost until the pointer enters another window.  Click-to-focus mode
can be enabled using the \fIfocus\fP resource.
.PP
A button 1 click on a window frame brings it to the top.  Dragging
button 1 on the frame of a resizable window repositions that edge of
the window.  If a corner rather than an edge is dragged, then both edges
forming the corner are repositioned.  While you're reshaping a window,
a little window pops up to show you the window's current size. Whether there is
a resize widget at the top of the title bar depends on Xresource config - see
below.
.PP
In the default configuration, button 1 on the root window does nothing.
.PP
Button 2 is used to drag a window by its frame, repositioning the window
but maintaining its position in the window stack.  This always just moves the
window, never resizing it.
.PP
In the default configuration, button 2 on the root window brings up a
new shell.
.PP
A button 3 click on a window frame hides that window.  Pressing
button 3 on the root window brings up a menu of all the windows.  Moving the
pointer over menu items while holding button 3 displays a coloured box
showing the window outline (even if it's hidden).
Releasing the button while over an item will unhide or raise the named window,
and give it focus.  The menu has two sections: the items above the dotted
line refer to hidden windows; those below to already visible windows.
.PP
A button 3 click in the frame while Shift is held down pushes the window
to the back, under any other windows.
.PP
A window may be closed by clicking on the little white cross in the top-left
corner of its frame.
.PP
Holding down the 'Windows' key (known to X as Super, or Mod4) turns the whole
of a window into window furniture, so that a window can be moved and resized
without going anywhere near its frame.  These gestures work on the window's
own background, and \fIlwm\fP swallows the clicks so that they never reach the
application.
.PP
Dragging button 1 with the Windows key held moves the window, wherever in it
the drag started, and raises it to the front, just as a button 1 press on its
frame does.  Clicking button 1 without moving the pointer raises the window
without moving it.
.PP
Dragging button 2 with the Windows key held resizes the window: which edge or
corner moves depends on where in the window the drag started, taking the
window to be divided into a 3x3 grid.  The corners of the grid drag the
corresponding corner of the window, the sides drag the corresponding edge,
and the middle square, which names no edge, moves the window instead.  That
move leaves the window where it is in the stacking order, so a window can be
nudged into place from behind another one; button 1's move is the one that
brings it to the front.
.PP
While a button 2 drag is going on, and with button 2 still held down, the
other two buttons still mean what they mean on their own.  Clicking button 1
raises the window, and the drag carries on; clicking button 3 hides the
window and abandons the drag, leaving the window as the drag had it so far.
As with every other click, moving the pointer away before releasing the
button abandons the click, and the drag simply goes on.
.PP
Double-clicking button 1 with the Windows key held expands the window instead:
the edge or corner picked out by the same 3x3 grid grows until it meets the
nearest window in its way, or the edge of the monitor, whichever comes first.
From the middle square, all four edges grow, so a double click in the middle
of an isolated window makes it as big as the space around it allows.  Double-
clicking button 2 does the same thing but ignores the other windows, so it
expands to the monitor - which, on a multi-monitor xrandr setup, means the
monitor that window is on, not the whole desktop.
.PP
The middle square is a toggle.  When there is nothing left for the window to
grow into, a double click there shrinks it back to the size it had before it
was expanded, so the same gesture both maximises a window and restores it.  A
window which is maximised because it asked to be is un-maximised instead.  A
window \fIlwm\fP has no earlier size recorded for - one which has never been
expanded, or which has already been restored - is left alone.
.PP
Clicking button 3 with the Windows key held hides the window, just as a
button 3 click on its frame does.  As on the frame, the click is abandoned if
the pointer moves away before the button is released.
.PP
Holding Control as well as the Windows key selects a different gesture
altogether: clicking button 1 turns \fIlwm\fP's furniture on or off for that
window.  A decorated window loses its title bar and borders, and grows into
the space they were using; an undecorated one - whether \fIlwm\fP never gave
it furniture, or the user took it away - gains them, and shrinks to make room.
Either way the window's outer extent stays exactly where it was, so nothing
else on the desktop has to move, and turning the furniture off and on again
leaves the window exactly the size it started at.  The gestures above keep
working on a window with no furniture, which is the only way such a window
can be moved at all.
As with the other clicks, moving the pointer away before releasing the button
abandons it.  A window which is full screen, or one of the window types
\fIlwm\fP never decorates (desktops, docks, menus and splash screens), is
left alone.
.PP
Holding the Windows key and pressing an arrow key moves the input focus to the
next window in that direction.  Windows are compared by the centres of their
frames: the window chosen is the nearest one whose centre lies within the cone
which starts at the centre of the focused window and opens out towards the
arrow pressed.  For \fBWindows+Left\fP that means a centre further left than
the focused window's, and no further away vertically than it is horizontally,
so a window which is mostly above or below the current one is not selected
however far to the left it also is.  A window lying exactly on the diagonal
belongs to both of the cones which meet there, which is what stops any window
being unreachable.
.PP
Windows on other monitors are included, so this is also the way to move the
focus from one screen to another.  Hidden windows are not included.  If there
is no window in the direction pressed, nothing happens at all.
.PP
The window which gains the focus is raised, and the pointer moves to it, so
that the mouse and the keyboard agree about which window is being worked on:
with the default focus-follows-mouse, a pointer left behind on another window
would hand the focus back at the first twitch of the mouse.  The pointer is
placed in the middle of the largest unbroken piece of the window which can
actually be seen - the biggest rectangle of it which is on screen, clear of
the space any panel has reserved, and not covered by anything still in front
of it.  Usually that is simply the middle of the window, since raising it has
put it in front of everything else.  What a raise does not get a window out
from under is its own dialogs, which come up with it and stay in front of it;
a window mostly covered by one of those gets the pointer in the middle of
whatever of it still shows, since dropping the pointer on the dialog would
give the dialog the focus straight back.  A window with nothing showing at all
leaves the pointer where it is.
.PP
A window is only kept in front for as long as it holds the focus.  When a
later press moves the focus on, the window it moves off goes back to the place
in the stack it had before it was raised, so that flipping between two windows
across a third leaves that third one where it was rather than stacking it on
top of both.  Any dialogs it owns go back down with it and stay in front of
it.  A window which has been moved by hand with \fBWindows+Shift\fP and an
arrow keeps its place at the front instead: having been put there on purpose,
it stays there.
.PP
Adding Shift moves the focused window itself rather than the focus.  The
window travels one step per press: the first takes it to the inner edge of the
monitor it is on, on the side the arrow names, keeping its position on the
other axis; once it is there, the next press hands it to the next monitor in
that direction, where it arrives against that monitor's near edge - the one it
has just crossed - so that it ends up beside where it was rather than jumping
the width of the new monitor.  The press after that carries it across to the
far side of its new monitor, and so on, so repeated presses walk a window
across the desk and each press undoes one press the other way.  When there is
no monitor that way, the window stays where it is.  A window which is
maximised stops being so, exactly as if it had been dragged with the mouse.
.PP
A window moved this way is raised, and if the pointer was on it, the pointer
travels with it and keeps its position on the window - proportionally, so that
a window which shrinks on the way still has the pointer over the same part of
itself.  Both of those exist to keep the focus where the user put it: with
sloppy focus, a window sliding out from under the pointer would hand the focus
to whatever was behind it, and a window arriving underneath another one would
hand it to that.  A pointer which was not on the window in the first place is
left alone.
.PP
A window too big for the monitor it arrives on is shrunk to fit it, on either
axis or both.  The same happens to a window dragged with the mouse onto a
monitor too small to hold it.  Nothing grows the window again afterwards,
except that a window which is dragged onto a small monitor and then, without
the button being released, back to the one it came from, gets its old size
back.  A window which was already too big for the monitor it was on - because
the application asked to be that big - is left at the size it chose.
.PP
A window which arrives with no position of its own is placed on the primary
monitor, cascading down and to the right of the last one.  The exception is a
monitor another program is running full screen on - a game, in practice.  A
window opened while that is up would be either hidden behind the game or
popped up over it, so it goes to the best of the monitors that are free
instead, and only stays on the primary if every monitor is taken or there is
only the one.  The full-screen program's own further windows are not moved:
they still open where it is.  Two windows count as one program's if either is
transient for the other, or if they share a \fBWM_CLIENT_LEADER\fP, a
\fBWM_HINTS\fP window group, or a \fB_NET_WM_PID\fP on the same
\fBWM_CLIENT_MACHINE\fP.
.PP
Both senses of "full screen" count: a window which asked for
\fB_NET_WM_STATE_FULLSCREEN\fP, and one which has simply sized itself to
cover a monitor exactly, which is what the borderless-fullscreen setting in a
game's display options produces.  A merely maximised window is neither.
.PP
It is possible to run commands when alt-clicking on a window's title bar.
The configured command is run with the window id (in hex with 0x prefix)
appended to it (separated by a space), so that the command run can be a script
that does some user-specified action to the window.
Separate commands can be configured for buttons 1 and 2. The default for
alt-button1 on the title bar is to run "force_title.sh", which can be found
alongside the LWM source code. This script allows the user to override a
window's visible title, so that annoying web apps that animate the window
title can be muted.
.SH OPTIONS
\fILwm\fP accepts the following command line options:
.PP
.TP 8
.B \-s
specifies a client ID for the X Session Management system, and is used
exclusively by session managers.
.TP 8
.B \-debugcli[=command1;command2...]
enables the debug CLI (command line interface), which allows LWM to be given
certain commands on stdin.  This is intended for debugging, not for normal LWM
use.  For details of commands available on the CLI, refer to the BUGS file.
If an '=commands' suffix is present, it is interpreted as a sequence of debugcli
commands, separated by ';', which LWM's debugcli will execute after startup is
complete.
.TP 8
.B \-test
runs all LWM's unit tests, exiting successfully if all tests pass.
.SH RESOURCES
\fILwm\fP understands the following X resources:
.TP 12
.B focus
if set to 'click', click-to-focus mode is activated, otherwise sloppy focus
.TP 12
.B titleFont
font used in window titles, and in the popup menu and size indicator
.TP 12
.B border
width in pixels of window borders
.TP 12
.B topBorder
how many of the top pixels of the title bar should be used as a resize widget.
Set this to zero to disable resizing on the title bar entirely (in which case,
the whole title bar, except the top-left and -right corners, will move the
window).
.TP 12
.B button1
program spawned when button 1 is clicked on the root window
.TP 12
.B button2
program spawned when button 2 is clicked on the root window
.TP 12
.B altButton1Title
program spawned when alt+button 1 is clicked on a window's title bar. The window
ID will be appended to this string (in hex with 0x prefix, separated with space)
and the resulting program will be run. Default value is "force_title.sh", which
for window 0x120002 will be run as "force_title.sh 0x120002".
.TP 12
.B altButton2Title
program spawned when alt+button 2 is clicked on a window's title bar. See the
description of altButton1Title for more info.
.TP 12
.B titleBGColour
background colour of the active window's title bar
.TP 12
.B borderColour
background colour of the active window's border
.TP 12
.B inactiveBorderColour
background colour of inactive windows' title bars and borders
.TP 12
.B windowHighlightColour
colour of the box showing window outlines from the window list menu
.TP 12
.B titleColour
colour of the title bar text in the active window
.TP 12
.B inactiveTitleColour
colour of the title bar text in inactive windows
.TP 12
.B closeIconColour
colour of the close icon in the active window
.TP 12
.B inactiveCloseIconColour
colour of the close icon in inactive windows
.TP 12
.B popupTextColour
text colour of the popup window (unhide menu and resizing popup)
.TP 12
.B popupBackgroundColour
background colour of the popup window
.TP 12
.B appIcon
where to show application icons. Possible values are 'none', 'title' (show only
in the app's window title bar), 'menu' (only in the unhide menu) or 'both'.
.TP 12
.B focusDelayMillis
how many milliseconds to delay repeated sloppy-focus focus events by. The
default is 50ms. If you find this is slow, reduce it. If you find that you can
move the mouse from window A to window B to window C, and sometimes have focus
remaining in window B, then increase this time a bit.
